history.idea:
Keep the current best pipeline, but use 3000-char windows and calibrate each threshold on the per-document minimum over windows, so the calibration matches the "every window must pass" rule. This differs from experiment 6, which used per-window percentiles and over-rejected long docs.

1. Windowing, the same function for target and pool docs: cut the full doc at 3000-char steps. If the last piece is shorter than 1500 chars, merge it into the previous window, so every window is 1500 to 4500 chars. A doc shorter than 3000 chars is one window.

2. Calibration: build the word and bigram sets from target docs 0..99, as now. For each of target docs 100..199, window the full text and compute big, voc and punct per window, then take the doc's minimum of each over its windows. Set thr_big = 0.9 * (5th percentile of the 100 doc-min big values), and likewise thr_voc = 0.97 * p5 of doc-min voc and thr_punct = 0.9 * p5 of doc-min punct. About 95% of clean docs then pass whatever their length.

3. Pool filter: a doc passes only if every window meets all three thresholds, with no failing-window allowance. doc_big = mean of the window big values.

4. Unchanged:
- zr bounds computed on doc[:6000], calibrated on target[:6000] as now (0.9 * min, 1.1 * max);
- nw >= 40 and rep <= 0.3;
- exact dedupe;
- title dedupe keeping the highest doc_big;
- budget logic: if over T drop the lowest doc_big, if under 0.5 T top up from docs whose first window passes in descending doc_big, otherwise cycle;
- final shuffle with random.Random(CURATE_SEED).

5. Speed: compute token counts once with enc.encode_ordinary_batch into a dict keyed by doc index, and do not use list.index.
